@lesson 01_basics
@title C++ basics
@summary Input and output with cout and cin, variables, pass by value and by reference, and how C++ differs from C.
@order 1

@section 1.1 First program
@points
- C++ is a compiled language like C; execution starts in main().
- #include <iostream> gives you cout (output) and cin (input).
- cout << value prints; << can be chained; endl ends the line.
- using namespace std; lets you write cout instead of std::cout.
- main returns 0 to signal success.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    cout << "Hello, World!" << endl;
    return 0;  // 0 means "everything worked correctly"
}
@output
Hello, World!

@section 1.2 Variables, types and arithmetic
@points
- Every variable must be declared with a type before use: int, double, char, bool.
- bool holds true or false; cout prints them as 1 and 0.
- const makes a variable unchangeable.
- int / int is integer division (10 / 3 is 3); use a double operand to keep the fraction. % gives the remainder.
- A variable declared without a value (int var;) is NOT initialised: never read it before assigning.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int age = 25;
    double pi = 3.14159;
    char grade = 'A';
    bool passed = true;
    const int MAX_SCORE = 100;

    cout << "Age: " << age << endl;
    cout << "Pi: " << pi << endl;
    cout << "Grade: " << grade << endl;
    cout << "Passed: " << passed << endl;      // bool prints as 1 or 0
    cout << "Max: " << MAX_SCORE << endl;
    cout << "10 / 3 = " << 10 / 3 << ", 10 % 3 = " << 10 % 3 << ", 10 / 3.0 = " << 10 / 3.0 << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output
Age: 25
Pi: 3.14159
Grade: A
Passed: 1
Max: 100
10 / 3 = 3, 10 % 3 = 1, 10 / 3.0 = 3.33333
@mistakes
- Using an uninitialised variable.
- Expecting 7 / 2 to be 3.5 (both operands are int, so the result is 3).

@section 1.3 Input with cin and pass by value
@points
- cin >> variable reads from the keyboard. Unlike scanf there is no format string and no & needed.
- Ordinary parameters are passed by value: the function receives a copy, so changing the parameter does not change the caller's variable.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int age;
    cout << "Enter your age: ";
    cin >> age;                    // cin takes the variable itself
    cout << "You are " << age << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output Example session (the user types 25)
Enter your age: 25
You are 25
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void tryToChange(int x) {
    x = 999;                    // only changes the local copy
    cout << "Inside function: x = " << x << endl;
}

int main() {
    int value = 5;
    cout << "Before call: value = " << value << endl;
    tryToChange(value);
    cout << "After call:  value = " << value << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output
Before call: value = 5
Inside function: x = 999
After call:  value = 5

@section 1.17 Pass by reference (c++ only)
@points
- C has no reference syntax; there you use pointers (see the Pointers lesson).
- In C++, a & in the parameter list passes the caller's variable itself; changes inside the function change the original.
- Uses: modify the caller's variable (swap), avoid copying large objects, "return" several values.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addOneByValue(int x) {         // works on a copy
    x = x + 1;
    cout << "Inside addOneByValue: x = " << x << endl;
}

void addOneByReference(int &x) {    // & = pass by reference: x is the caller's variable
    x = x + 1;
    cout << "Inside addOneByReference: x = " << x << endl;
}

void swapInts(int &a, int &b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    cout << "a before: " << a << endl;
    addOneByValue(a);
    cout << "After addOneByValue:     a = " << a << endl;
    addOneByReference(a);
    cout << "After addOneByReference: a = " << a << endl;

    int p = 1, q = 2;
    swapInts(p, q);
    cout << "After swap: p = " << p << ", q = " << q << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output 
a before: 5
Inside addOneByValue: x = 6
After addOneByValue:     a = 5
Inside addOneByReference: x = 6
After addOneByReference: a = 6
After swap: p = 2, q = 1

@section 1.5 From C to C++
@points
- Output: printf("%d\n", x) becomes cout << x << endl.
- Input: scanf("%d", &x) becomes cin >> x.
- Headers: <stdio.h> becomes <iostream>.
- Pointers: NULL becomes nullptr.
- Dynamic memory: malloc and free become new and delete (new[] and delete[] for arrays).
- Modifying a caller's variable: pointer parameters still work, but a reference parameter (int &x) is cleaner.
- Data and behaviour together: struct (data only) grows into class with methods, constructors and access control.
@code text
C                          C++
printf("%d\n", x);         cout << x << endl;
scanf("%d", &x);           cin >> x;
#include <stdio.h>         #include <iostream>
NULL                       nullptr
malloc(n * sizeof(int))    new int[n]
free(p)                    delete[] p
void f(int *p)  f(&x)      void f(int &r)  f(x)
struct Point { ... };      class Point { public: ... };
